209:#### Numeric semantics
210-
211-A `number` is a 64-bit float. Every operation on numbers is **total**: it either
212-returns a number or fails with a runtime error. There is no third outcome in
213-which an operation returns something that is not a number.
214-
215-| Expression | Outcome |
216-|---|---|
217-| `1 / 0`, `0 / 0`, `-1 / 0` | runtime error: `Division by zero` |
218-| `5 % 0`, `5 % 0.0`, `5 % -0.0` | runtime error: `Modulo by zero` |
219-| `-0.0` | the number `0`: negative zero is not a distinct value |
220-| `1e308 * 1e308` | runtime error: `infinity is not a finite number` |
221-| `1e400` (a literal too large to hold) | runtime error: `infinity is not a finite number` |
222-| `-5 % 3` | `-2`: a remainder takes the sign of the dividend |
223-
224-Three consequences are worth stating plainly:
225-
226-1. **A number is always finite.** `NaN`, `infinity` and `-infinity` cannot
227-   enter a `number`, from arithmetic, from a literal, from `json.parse`, or from
228-   a library function. There is no way to obtain one, so there is no value whose
229-   comparisons are all false.
230-2. **Whole numbers are exact only to 2^53.** `9007199254740993` reads as
231-   `9007199254740992`. A whole number wider than 2^53 is printed in full rather
232-   than through a 64-bit integer, so it never reports a number the program does
233-   not hold.
234-3. **Non-finite numbers still print.** A number that is not finite can only be
235-   built by a host program embedding Redblue, and it prints as `not a number`,
236-   `infinity` or `negative infinity`. `json.stringify` of one is `null`, which is
237-   what a JSON writer emits for it.
238-
239-### Comparison
240-
241-```redblue
242-// Text comparison
243-if name is "Alice"
244-if x is greater than 10
245-if x is less than or equal to 100
246-
247-// Alternative operators
248-if x > 10          // is greater than
249-if x >= 10         // is greater than or equal to
250-if x < 10          // is less than
251-if x <= 10         // is less than or equal to
252-if x == 10         // is equal to
253-if x != 10         // is not
254-```
